<?xml version="1.0"?>
<model>
  <name>std_cargo_container_40ft_on_wheels</name>
  <version>1.0</version>
  <sdf version='1.7'>model.sdf</sdf>

  <author>
    <name>Juan Francisco Rascón Crespo</name>
    <email>juan.rascon@eurecat.org</email>
  </author>
  <description>
    A standard 40-foot cargo container is modeled, intended for ship or trailer transport. The outer dimensions of the
    container are 12.19 m (length) × 2.336 m (width) × 2.413 m (height), while the internal cargo space measures 12.01 m ×
    2.33 m × 2.38 m. The unloaded (tare) mass of the container is 3701 kg, and it is designed to carry a maximum payload
    of 26780 kg.
    The container is mounted on a chassis structure that provides mechanical support and interfaces with the ground via
    four wheels (two axles). The chassis serves as the structural frame between the wheel assemblies and the container
    base. It elevates the container such that the cargo floor (i.e., the bottom internal surface of the container) is
    positioned at a height of exactly 1.20 m above ground level, which is consistent with standard trailer loading dock
    height.
    This configuration reflects a typical road-ready container-trailer unit as used in intermodal freight transport
    systems.
  </description>
</model>
